const weekOneSlides = {"unit":1,"week":1,"licence":"CC BY 4.0","codeLicence":"MIT","chapter":"start","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#start","references":["https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#input","https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#int","https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#print"],"lessons":[{"id":"u1-w01-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","prepare":"Whiteboard or paper; chapter open. No Python installation needed for this lesson.","slides":[{"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 1","Explain the problem before writing code."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Introduce the week: plan, run, trace/test, then apply. This lesson is diagnostic and formative."},{"title":"What does a program need?","body":["A club sells badges for $3 each.","What must we know to calculate an order?","How would you find the cost of four badges?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Think-pair-share. Expected: quantity is needed; multiply four by three to obtain $12. Ask how the same method works for a different quantity. Do not assume prior coding experience."},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Identify input, processing and output.","Write ordered steps for a calculation.","Calculate an expected result before testing."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success criteria: identify quantity as input, multiplication as processing and displayed total as output; give a correct expected result for a new quantity. Distinguish a variable quantity from the stated fixed price."},{"title":"The badge requirement","body":["Display the cost for a valid badge quantity.","Each badge costs $3.","Assume a whole-number quantity of zero or more.","Input validation is taught later."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Ask what valid means here. Zero is allowed. Negative quantities, words and decimal text are outside this introductory assumption; do not claim this is a finished robust application."},{"title":"Input, processing and output","table":[["Stage","Badge example"],["Input","Quantity: 4"],["Processing","4 × 3 = 12"],["Output","Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model one row at a time verbally. Learners then annotate the same stages for quantity 6. Expected total: $18. The number read is not the displayed answer."},{"title":"Write an algorithm","body":["1. Read the badge quantity.","2. Multiply the quantity by 3.","3. Display the total.","Now trace the steps for 0, 1 and 6 badges."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Independent paper task, then compare reasoning. Expected totals: $0, $3 and $18. An algorithm is an ordered method; Python will be one way to implement it. Ask why displaying the result before calculating it would fail."},{"title":"Check the plan","body":["Does it accept the required input?","Does it calculate the right result?","Does it display that result?","Compare with an independently calculated expectation."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Use a deliberately incorrect rule, quantity + 3, for six badges: it produces 9 rather than 18. A plan that produces output can still be wrong. Keep corrected input/process/output notes as evidence."},{"title":"Exit check: seven badges","body":["Name the input.","State the processing step.","Give the expected displayed total.","Explain why an expected result is useful."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected: quantity 7; multiply by 3; Total: $21. Expected results provide an independent correctness check. Reteach any confusion between reading input and processing before the next lesson."}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Run your first Python script","prepare":"School-approved Python 3 editor/IDE, writable practice folder and projector. Check the school’s Run command before class.","slides":[{"title":"Run your first Python script","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 2","Turn the badge algorithm into a saved program."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Use the school-approved environment. Do not ask learners to install software or change school settings during the lesson."},{"title":"Retrieve the badge plan","body":["What is the input?","Which instruction calculates the total?","What should six badges cost?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected: quantity; quantity multiplied by 3; $18. Use quick responses to decide whether a short input/process/output reminder is needed."},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Save and run a Python script.","Enter suitable data when prompted.","Compare actual output with a prediction."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: the learner saves badge_cost.py, runs it independently, identifies where to enter the quantity and records a matching prediction/result."},{"title":"Prepare the workspace","body":["1. Open the approved Python editor or IDE.","2. Create a folder for this week’s work.","3. Save a new file as badge_cost.py.","4. Locate the editor, Run command and output area."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Demonstrate using the actual school environment. An IDE combines tools for writing and running code. Avoid a filename such as random.py that could shadow a standard Python module. Saving and running are separate actions."},{"title":"The badge script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10,"notes":"Read top to bottom. input reads text, int converts suitable whole-number text, assignment binds the calculated value to total, and print displays it. Uppercase BADGE_PRICE communicates intent to keep the price fixed; Python does not enforce that convention."},{"title":"Predict, save, run","body":["Predict the result for four badges.","Save the file, then use Run.","Enter 4 when the program asks.","Record the expected and actual output."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Expected: Total: $12. Help learners distinguish the source editor from the program’s input/output area. Ask them to rerun with 1 and 0, predicting first: $3 and $0. Do not use invalid inputs for this introductory task."},{"title":"Make one controlled change","body":["Change BADGE_PRICE from 3 to 5.","Predict the total for four badges.","Save and rerun the program.","Explain which line changed and why."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected total $20. If output is unchanged, check saving and which file is running before editing more code. Return the price to 3 after checking."},{"title":"Exit check: run independently","body":["Show your saved Python file.","Run it for two badges at $3 each.","State the expected output before running.","Explain the difference between Save and Run."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected Total: $6. Save stores changes; Run executes the program. Record environment/setup difficulties for support rather than treating every failure as a conceptual programming error."}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Trace, modify and test a sequence","prepare":"Working badge_cost.py at price 3; paper trace table or worksheet; Python editor.","slides":[{"title":"Trace, modify and test","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 3","Explain what changes as each instruction runs."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Learners should already be able to run the badge script. Keep a clean copy available so setup errors do not consume the tracing lesson."},{"title":"Retrieve the script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","body":["Predict the output for quantity 5."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected Total: $15. Ask which line performs the calculation and which displays its result. Let learners predict before running."},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Trace variable values in execution order.","Modify a calculation to match a requirement.","Record expected and actual results."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: complete a trace for a new input, add a fixed charge correctly, and explain at least one meaningful test. A trace records state after each instruction, not just the final answer."},{"title":"Trace four badges","table":[["After instruction","Value known"],["BADGE_PRICE = 3","BADGE_PRICE is 3"],["Read quantity 4","quantity is 4"],["Calculate total","total is 12"],["Display total","Output: Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model this trace, then give quantity 2 for independent completion. total has not been assigned before its calculation line. int(input(...)) reads and converts the entered text before binding quantity."},{"title":"Add a fixed packaging charge","code":"PACKAGING = 2\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + PACKAGING","body":["The $2 charge applies to every order, including zero badges.","Predict the totals for quantities 0 and 3."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Expected $2 and $11 at badge price 3. State the zero-order rule explicitly: a different requirement could omit the charge, but this version includes it. Multiplication is evaluated before addition here; detailed operator work follows next week."},{"title":"Test the modified program","table":[["Quantity","Expected","Actual"],["0","$2","Record your run"],["1","$5","Record your run"],["3","$11","Record your run"]],"minutes":12,"notes":"Learners implement the modification and run each case. Ask them to keep the expected results unchanged if actual output differs; investigate the calculation instead. For a fixed charge, quantity * (3 + 2) is a useful contrasting fault: it charges packaging per badge."},{"title":"A program can run and be wrong","body":["Syntax error: Python cannot parse the instructions.","Logic error: the instructions produce the wrong result.","Compare quantity × 3 + 2 with quantity × (3 + 2)."],"minutes":5,"notes":"For quantity 3, the intended expression gives 11 while the second gives 15. Neither expression is a syntax error. Have learners explain the requirement in words before fixing the expression."},{"title":"Exit check: a changed order","body":["Badges cost $3 each; packaging is $2 per order.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Name one useful zero/boundary test.","Explain how you know the calculation is correct."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected $20. Quantity 0 should give $2 under the explicit rule. Look for an independently calculated expectation and a test record, not simply ‘it ran’."}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","prepare":"Python editor, a new cover_cost.py file and a test-record template. Keep the badge example available as a reference.","slides":[{"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 4","Plan, implement, test and explain a new problem."],"minutes":2,"notes":"This is formative transfer practice, not an additional summative assessment. Let learners use their earlier notes while making their own design and test decisions."},{"title":"Retrieve a useful test habit","body":["Why predict output before running?","How does a trace help find a mistake?","What is the difference between a fixed charge and a price per item?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected: independent expectation checks correctness; a trace locates the first wrong value; per-item cost varies with quantity while a fixed charge is added once. Use a small numerical example if needed."},{"title":"The book-cover requirement","body":["Each book cover costs $2.","Every order has a $4 handling charge.","The handling charge also applies to a zero-cover order.","Assume the quantity is a whole number of zero or more."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success criteria: correct input/process/output plan, readable saved script, three independently predicted tests, and an explanation of one line. Validation and conditional zero-order charging are beyond this task."},{"title":"Design before coding","body":["Input: what must the user enter?","Processing: write the calculation in words.","Output: what should the program display?","Choose meaningful names for the two fixed values."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Expected input quantity; calculation quantity × 2 + 4; output total cost. Suggested constants COVER_PRICE and HANDLING. Ask learners to draft their own sequence before opening a completed reference solution."},{"title":"Calculate expected results","table":[["Covers","Your working","Expected total"],["0","0 × 2 + 4","$4"],["1","1 × 2 + 4","$6"],["5","5 × 2 + 4","$14"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Reveal/model the first row verbally, then ask learners to cover the results and calculate all three independently. If the projector shows the full table, add a fresh quantity such as 3 ($10) to check independent reasoning."},{"title":"Implement your plan","body":["Create and save cover_cost.py.","Read and convert the quantity.","Calculate the total using your named values.","Display a clearly labelled result; run your three tests."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Reference: COVER_PRICE = 2; HANDLING = 4; quantity = int(input('Covers: ')); total = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING; print(f'Total: ${total}'). Scaffold with the badge example or a partially completed file; extension: change a fixed value and design a fresh test."},{"title":"Explain your evidence","body":["Show expected and actual results side by side.","If a result differs, find the first wrong step.","Explain why the handling charge is added once.","State one assumption or limitation."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Check quantities 0, 1 and 5 against $4, $6 and $14. Suitable limitation: invalid text and negative quantities are not handled. Do not equate a plausible output with successful validation."},{"title":"Exit check: a fresh quantity","body":["Predict the cost of three book covers.","Identify one input, processing and output step.","Explain why this test is independent of the program.","Save your script and test evidence."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected $10. An independent prediction uses the stated rule rather than copying the program’s output. Next week builds on types, arithmetic and data representation. Identify learners needing setup or tracing support before moving on."}]}]};
